          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is osteoarthritis not simply “wear and tear” of a joint?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is not just a joint slowly wearing out. It affects the whole joint, including the cartilage, the bone underneath, the ligaments, the joint lining and the muscles around it. Cartilage is lost over time, and the bone beneath it reshapes in response (*Osteoarthritis*, condition summary).\n\nThe process is also active rather than purely mechanical. It involves inflammation and attempts at repair (*Osteoarthritis*, condition summary). This matters because the \"wear and tear\" idea often leads people to rest a painful joint. Muscle weakness from reduced use can then make the joint worse, not protect it. That is why exercise is the first-line treatment (*Osteoarthritis*, condition summary; *Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management*, guideline summary).\n\nThe guideline says exercise helps regardless of how severe the joint looks on imaging, age, or how much pain someone starts with. It also says that pain within tolerable limits during exercise is not a sign of damage. Supervision at the start helps people do the right movements and progress safely (*Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management*, guideline summary). The programme usually combines muscle strengthening, range-of-movement work and aerobic activity (*Exercising with osteoarthritis*, lifestyle summary).\n\nThe sources I searched did not cover the causes of osteoarthritis in detail, or how it is diagnosed. If you have a joint that hurts or is getting worse, a clinician can assess it and help you plan what to do next.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
